ID: B3-Ch12b-Distracting
TEXT:

Flower and Melindra's exchange over the transceiver stretches past fifteen minutes, and naturally I get dragged in as a fellow connoisseur of geological gravel. The moment the mineral debate starts, Daren and Kate drop their pace, letting half a dozen paces open between us on the pine-needle trail so they can talk about anything else under the sun.

I could drop back too and leave Flower to it, but I lack the heart. Or I have developed an unhealthy tolerance for quartz analysis.

"So," Kate cuts in from five paces back, her voice tightening with tactical focus, "are we addressing the four mages heading straight toward us, or are we pretending they are part of the scenery?"

"Every party we felt over the past two days steered clear once they caught our auras," Daren says, his hand resting lightly on his hilt. "No reason to assume these four are different."

"They are," Kate says, dark eyes fixed on the treeline. "They felt us and adjusted their angle. They are intercepting. At this pace, we meet them in two minutes."

"Right, I am hanging up before I get you killed," Melindra's tinny voice crackles from the transceiver stone in Flower's palm. "Stay safe, Flower."

"You too, Melindra! Bye!" Flower snaps the connection shut and tucks the stone into her sash.

"I can take to the air and scout them from above before we collide," I say, glancing back at Daren.

"Barry," Daren says, holding up a steady hand. "I know you want to test the flight currents outside drills, but not here. You won't learn more from the canopy than our magical sense already tells us, and if they have long-range artillery, you are an open target in the sky. We stay together."

choice("Insist on going to scout the mages.", B3-Ch12b-Scout, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Scout, v_b3_ch12_scout = 1)
choice("Agree with Daren and stay with the group.", B3-Ch12b-Necromancers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Necromancers, v_b3_ch12_scout = 0)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Scout
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_scout,0) if (v_b3_ch11_red == 0 || v_b3_ch11_red == 1 || v_perception < 2)
set(v_b3_ch12_scout,2) if (v_perception == 2)
set(v_b3_ch12_scout,3) if (v_perception >= 3)
 

"Yeah, but maybe I could tell what kind of mages they are from their vestments and gear," I say. "That gives us a minute to plan our counters before the vanguard meets. If you're worried about an ambush, I'll keep my ceiling high and stay sharp on my surroundings. It'll be fine."

"I could go with him!" Flower says, bouncing on her toes with a bright grin. "Two heads are better than none!"

"That is not how the saying goes..." Daren mutters, rubbing his brow with a weary sigh. "And between the canopy and the distance, you'll see patches of cloth at best. But go. Just keep it short. If this is a fight, we meet it together, not in pieces."

"Aye, aye, sir!" Flower says, kicking off the grass as twin plumes of bright flame erupt under her boots. "Let's go, Barry!"

"Right behind you," I say, wrapping my air currents around my torso and lifting off after her into the canopy.

#if(v_b3_ch11_red == 0) {
The lift is jerky, wobbling beneath my ribs as I rise through the pine branches. Two days of drills smoothed out the violent lurches from the underground base, but with only a single point in my magical stats, the currents fight my body weight rather than glide. Melindra's wind work was second nature; mine still feels like balancing on a greased plank over a chasm. Another point or two in power will smooth the drag.

"Over there!" Flower calls back, banking through the humid air above the canopy. "Straight ahead in the clearing!"

"Keep your ceiling," I call over the rush of wind. "Basic projectile spells carry immense vertical range. We stay outside their direct arc."

We level off high above the open patch of ground, keeping well out of range for anything short of a dedicated long-distance artillery caster. I have no idea how sharp their vertical magical sense runs, but the answer comes fast.

"Hey, look, that guy is waving at us!" Flower says, waving both arms back down at the figures below. "Do you think they're friendly?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_red == 1) {
The air takes my weight with clean, immediate lift. Even with a single point in my magical stats, the principle I pulled from the Magium node keeps the air pressure locked beneath my boots in smooth, responsive drafts, almost indistinguishable from Melindra's effortless slipstreams.

"Over there!" Flower calls back, banking through the humid air above the canopy. "Straight ahead in the clearing!"

"Keep your ceiling," I call over the rush of wind. "Basic projectile spells carry immense vertical range. We stay outside their direct arc."

We level off high above the open patch of ground, keeping well out of range for anything short of a dedicated long-distance artillery caster. I have no idea how sharp their vertical magical sense runs, but the answer comes fast.

"Hey, look, that guy is waving at us!" Flower says, waving both arms back down at the figures below. "Do you think they're friendly?"

}
#if(v_perception < 2) {
"Well, I'm certainly not dropping down to find out," I say, squinting through the glare. "Three of them are dressed in black robes and the fourth is in emerald green, but from this height I can't make out insignia. What about you?"

"Uh... I see that they're not shooting fireballs at us, so I guess that's good?..." Flower says.

"They're standing dead center in a wide clearing with zero cover, waiting for us to walk into it," I say. "We head back before their hospitality expires."

"Agreed," Illuna's dry voice cuts through Flower's throat. "We have the layout. Lingering achieves nothing."

}
#if(v_perception == 2) {
"Well, I'm certainly not dropping down to find out," I say, squinting through the glare. "From the cut of their vestments, three are disciples of the God of Death, and the one in green with the circlet is a follower of Fate. Beyond that, nothing stands out. What about you?"

"Uh... I see that they're not shooting fireballs at us, so I guess that's good?..." Flower says.

"They're standing dead center in a wide clearing with zero cover, waiting for us to walk into it," I say. "We head back before their hospitality expires."

"Agreed," Illuna's dry voice cuts through Flower's throat. "We have the layout. Lingering achieves nothing."

}
#if(v_perception >= 3) {
"Well, I'm certainly not dropping down to find out," I say, squinting through the glare. "Three disciples of the God of Death, and one follower of Fate in green robes. And for a split second, I caught the illusionist burying something in the turf near the tree line before stepping back. What about you? Notice anything else?"

"Uh... I see that they're not shooting fireballs at us, so I guess that's good?..." Flower says.

"They're standing dead center in a wide clearing with zero cover, waiting for us to walk into it," I say. "We head back before their hospitality expires."

"Agreed," Illuna's dry voice cuts through Flower's throat. "We have the layout. Lingering achieves nothing."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Necromancers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Necromancers)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Necromancers
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_scout == 0) {
"You make a good point," I say. "We stay together and see what unfolds."

"It is possible their sensory range is narrow and they haven't caught our signature yet," Daren says. "We hold our heading for a minute or two and watch how they react."

We keep moving at a steady march. Over the next two minutes, the four approaching auras grind to a dead halt directly in our path. They are setting an ambush, or waiting for us to walk into their perimeter. We pull up thirty paces out beneath the shade of the pines to confer.

"Thoughts?" Daren asks, resting a hand on his pommel. "Are they looking for a duel, or looking to talk?"

"Auras don't give motives," Kate says, her eyes narrowed. "We don't even know if we've crossed paths before."

"You haven't," Arraka's muffled voice cuts in from the amulet. "I would remember."

"Then we have no baseline," Kate says. "Hostiles, scavengers, or tourists asking for pinecone coordinates. We find out when we reach them."

"We could skirt around them," Diane suggests. "It adds half an hour to the trek, but we still make the sacred woods."

"Not a chance!" Hadrik's voice booms through the trees. "I haven't had a proper brawl since Ollendor outside of Daren's gentle sparring matches, and you want me to turn tail? If these four want a fight, they get one!"

"Avoiding them is pointless anyway," Kate says. "We feel them, they feel us. If we skirt wide, they shadow our flank. Better to meet them head-on."

"Hah!" Hadrik grins. "That is more like it."

"If we are in agreement, forward," Daren says, scanning the treeline ahead. "Keep your eyes sharp. They've had ample time to prepare the clearing."

We push through the final line of brush and step out into a wide, sunlit expanse of cleared turf.

Three of the figures wear pitch-black robes, their shaved scalps inked with interlocking serpent glyphs, mirror images of Zack, the necromancer from our first week on the island. The fourth wears emerald robes and a slender silver circlet: an initiate of the God of Fate.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_scout == 1) {
Flower waves a cheery goodbye to the mage who waved at her, and we bank hard, dropping back through the canopy to rejoin the group in seconds. The moment our boots hit the grass, we brief Daren on the open-field setup.

Daren rubs his chin, considering. "Waiting in the open without hostility. What do you think? Friendly, or setting an ambush?"

"No way to know without speaking to them," Kate says. "Barry's description doesn't match anyone we've met, so motives are anyone's guess."

"We could skirt around them," Diane suggests. "It adds half an hour to the trek, but we still make the sacred woods."

"Not a chance!" Hadrik's voice booms through the trees. "I haven't had a proper brawl since Ollendor outside of Daren's gentle sparring matches, and you want me to turn tail? If these four want a fight, they get one!"

"Avoiding them is pointless anyway," Kate says. "We feel them, they feel us. If we skirt wide, they shadow our flank. Better to meet them head-on."

"Hah!" Hadrik grins. "That is more like it."

"If we are in agreement, forward," Daren says, scanning the treeline ahead. "Keep your eyes sharp. They've had ample time to prepare the clearing."

We push through the final line of brush and step out into the wide clearing where the four mages wait.

Up close, the three in black robes carry identical serpent tattoos across their shaven heads, unmistakable disciples of the God of Death like Zack. The fourth in green robes with the silver circlet is a follower of the God of Fate.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_scout == 2) {
Flower waves a cheery goodbye to the mage who waved at her, and we bank hard, dropping back through the canopy to rejoin the group in seconds. The moment our boots hit the grass, we brief Daren on the open-field setup.

Daren rubs his chin, considering. "Three Death followers and one Fate disciple waiting in an open field. One waves, neither attacks. What do you think? Friendly, or setting an ambush?"

"No way to know without speaking to them," Kate says. "Barry's description doesn't match anyone we've met, so motives are anyone's guess."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_scout == 3) {
Flower waves a cheery goodbye to the mage who waved at her, and we bank hard, dropping back through the canopy to rejoin the group in seconds. The moment our boots hit the grass, we brief Daren on the open-field setup.

Daren rubs his chin, considering. "Three Death followers and one Fate disciple. One waves, one plants something in the turf, neither attacks. What do you think? Friendly, or setting an ambush?"

"No way to know without speaking to them," Kate says. "Barry's description doesn't match anyone we've met, so motives are anyone's guess."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_scout == 2 || v_b3_ch12_scout == 3) {
"We could skirt around them," Diane suggests. "It adds half an hour to the trek, but we still make the sacred woods."

"Not a chance!" Hadrik's voice booms through the trees. "I haven't had a proper brawl since Ollendor outside of Daren's gentle sparring matches, and you want me to turn tail? If these four want a fight, they get one!"

"Avoiding them is pointless anyway," Kate says. "We feel them, they feel us. If we skirt wide, they shadow our flank. Better to meet them head-on."

"Hah!" Hadrik grins. "That is more like it."

"If we are in agreement, forward," Daren says, scanning the treeline ahead. "Keep your eyes sharp. They've had ample time to prepare the clearing."

We push through the final line of brush and step out into the wide clearing where the four mages wait.

Just as I spotted from above, three disciples of Death stand in black vestments with serpent tattoos across their scalps, exact replicas of Zack from our early days in Varathia. Beside them stands the Fate mage in green robes and a polished silver circlet.

}
Before Daren can call out across the grass, the lead necromancer steps forward and raises his voice.

"Before we begin," the necromancer sneers, "congratulations on actually walking into our range after sensing our overwhelming magical power! Most mortals soil themselves at our aura, so credit where it's due for raw bravado."

"You cannot be serious," Daren says, stepping up with his shield resting easy against his forearm. "Our aura dwarfs yours, and we outnumber you. Did your magical sense burn out on the road?"

The three in black burst into matching barks of laughter before the one in front raises a hand, cutting them off.

"The only thing clear," the necromancer says, "is that your tiny minds cannot grasp the gulf between devout servants of our god and faithless wretches muttering incantations from parchment. But you amuse me. Hand over the little girl without a fight, and I let the rest of you walk away with your skin."

"What?" Daren's brow furrows in anger. "Why? What business do you have with Flower?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Hostage, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Hostage)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Hostage
TEXT:

"The God of Death requires her as a hostage against the stillwater called Eiden," the lead necromancer says. "Once we finish with her, if she still draws breath, we might toss her back to you."

"Why would your deity on the magical plane concern himself with an earthen stillwater?" Diane asks, stepping forward. "And why would Eiden care if Flower lives or dies? I wasn't aware they had any connection."

"Have you been buried in a cave for three days?" the necromancer snaps. "Do you not know a WAR is tearing the magical plane apart as we speak?"

"A war?" Diane feigns wide-eyed shock. "Goodness, no idea! But that still doesn't explain what Eiden has to do with it."

"Our god knows from absolute authority that Eiden sparked this conflict," the necromancer says. "The God of Death has marked him for slaughter. But manifesting on this plane limits our master's full power. That is why he needs the girl. The golden fox bound Eiden by oath never to kill her. Since she still breathes, she has value as a hostage. Hand her over and keep your blood inside your veins."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"So the revenant failed, and now Death sends hirelings," Daren says, his voice flat. "Does your god truly believe Eiden values anyone's life?"

"Silence, dog!" the lead necromancer snarls. "Be grateful for the mercy I offer. Give us the girl and walk away."

"How many times must I endure your pathetic bargaining before you do what needs to be done, you coward?" the follower of Fate cuts in, speaking for the first time.

"You dare speak to me like that, madman?" the necromancer rounds on him. "After I granted you safe passage on this hunt?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"The fox told Eiden not to kill me?" Flower blinks. "I had no idea!"

"She shields us from the shadows," Illuna's voice remarks. "Twenty years of pulling injured beasts out of snares apparently bought some goodwill."

"They're my friends! Why wouldn't I help them?" Flower protests.

"Right," Illuna murmurs.

"Quiet, wretches!" the lead necromancer shouts. "I did not march out here to listen to nursery chatter! Surrender the girl or bleed where you stand!"

"How many times must I endure your pathetic bargaining before you do what needs to be done, you coward?" the follower of Fate cuts in, speaking for the first time.

"You dare speak to me like that, madman?" the necromancer rounds on him. "After I granted you safe passage on this hunt?"

}
"You would never have tracked them without my scrying," the Fate follower says, his eyes wild behind the silver circlet. "Your god grants no distant vision. I am the only one actively hunting this company, and you promised to help me exterminate them."

"Everyone knows your brain is rotted, Clogworth," the necromancer spits. "I had no intention of keeping promises to a lunatic."

"You admit to my face that you lied?" Clogworth steps closer, trembling with rage.

"Can I kill them now," Kate whispers, leaning toward Daren, "or are we politely waiting for their lovers' quarrel to conclude?"

"Give me a second," Daren whispers back, eyes fixed on the turf between us. "Checking for aura signatures. Making sure they didn't lace the field with traps."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Visions, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Visions)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Visions
TEXT:

"No traps, blockhead," Arraka's voice crackles from the amulet. "I'd have told you."

"Right," Daren mutters. "Still adjusting to you actually contributing instead of laughing while we bleed."

"They are not delusions, you parasite!" Clogworth shrieks at his ally, his voice rising over the clearing. "They are divine revelations! My mind connects directly to my god—his subconscious leaks into my sleep! I saw them! I saw these fools standing before Eiden, begging him to spare the cities of Varathia! That is not his destiny! He is the bringer of ruin! These mortals and that cursed fox are the only anchors keeping him from casting off the last shred of his humanity and fulfilling his purpose! They must die!"

"How deluded do you have to be to think you read a deity's thoughts in your sleep?" the lead necromancer laughs, shaking his head. "You bring zero proof, rave about dreams, and expect obedience. You are utterly insane."

A cold prickle runs down my spine. The lunatic actually dreamed of our private exchange with Eiden about sparing the cities. If his other sleep-visions carry that same precision, we are lucky the necromancers dismiss him as a raving clown.

"Do I fire or not?" Kate whispers again, frost swirling around the tips of her fingers. "Say the word, and I bury them in ice."

choice("Tell Kate to attack them.", B3-Ch12b-Attack, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Attack)
choice("Tell Kate to wait for them to make their move.", B3-Ch12b-Wait, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Wait)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Wait
TEXT:

"Hold one second," I whisper to Kate. "Let's see what move they make."

"Bad idea," Kate mutters, tracking the mages as they argue.

"Do you not see what they are doing?" Clogworth shrieks, pointing upward. "While we bickered, they gathered frost in the sky! Look up! They're going to impale us!"

"As if the God of Death bows to hail," the lead necromancer sneers, raising both arms to the sky. "Behold! Power none of you could ever hope to attain—the might of a true god!"

The ground trembles violently. Around our perimeter, a hundred bleached skeletons tear their way through the sod, an aura of suffocating magnitude pouring off every single ribcage.

Kate unleashes her volley instantly, sending the prepared icicles hurtling toward the four mages, but a skeleton steps into the path and throws up a shimmering barrier that deflects the entire spread.

"Blast it!" Kate grits her teeth. "Should have fired sooner."

"Skeleton stillwaters again," Daren says, thrusting his sword forward to snap a dome-shaped white ward around us. "Barry, how long for the device to draw a charge off this many signatures?"

"With a hundred of them?" I tap the screen. "Under a minute."

"Reinforce me!" Daren shouts over the thunderous hammering of spells against the ward. "I need sixty seconds to get Barry's emergency boost online before we push out!"

"Clearing the front first," Kate says, leveling her scepter. Four massive frost lances condense in front of the gem and streak forward, obliterating the skeleton's shield and slamming dead center into the four mages.

The lances pass clean through their chests as if striking mist, embedding harmlessly in the turf behind them.

"Illusions!" Arraka's shriek pierces the roar of combat. "They were phantoms from the start!"

"Damn it, Arraka!" Daren roars, leaning his shoulder into the buckling shield ward. "You have the sharpest magical sense on the continent! How did you miss an illusion matrix?!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Wait2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Wait2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Wait2
TEXT:

"Shut up!" Arraka snarls. "Their auras felt solid down to the marrow! That green-robed lunatic must have pulled one of Memphir's master weaves and primed it before we entered the sector! It's the only explanation!"

"Guilty!" Clogworth bellows across the clearing, his voice carrying over the barrage of spells pounding our dome. "Not even the legendary Arraka pierces a weave direct from the God of Fate! Just one of many revelations whispered into my dreams!"

"The wretch was born with an open channel to Memphir," Arraka tells us, voice dripping poison. "Instead of a broken link like Daren's, he got the unobstructed pipe. Argh! Why Memphir? His spell-knots are a nightmare to untangle!"

"Meaning you can't undo it," Illuna says.

"I can!" Arraka snaps. "It takes time! If you survive a hundred stillwaters while I work the knot, I'll hand you the counter!"

"Any progress on Emergency Mode, Barry?" Daren calls out.

"Nothing," I say, staring at the blank slate on my stat device. "The draw is dead. These skeletons are crafted tighter than Zack's—they barely bleed any ambient charge, even in a swarm."

"Kate, ice dome," Daren calls out, sweat beading on his forehead. "I can't hold this white ward on my own."

"On it," Kate says, locking her hands together as a thick shell of translucent ice encases Daren's barrier.

"Will the emergency stats trigger, or do we break out?" Daren asks.

"At this bleed rate, the charge won't trip," I say. "How is the shell holding?"

"Solid," Daren says. "Despite the terrifying aura signature, their impacts carry almost no mass. They aren't even scratching the frost."

"Maybe they aren't real stillwaters?" I mutter. "Or saving their mana for a heavy strike. What if—"

A deafening roar rips through the earth beneath us. Blinding fire and concussive thunder swallow the dome whole, tearing through frost and flesh before thought can even form. Darkness takes me instantly.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Attack
TEXT:

"Yes," I whisper to Kate. "Drop them while their mouths are moving."

"Say no more," Kate murmurs, and the swarm of icicles hovering above the canopy drops like iron bolts, driving straight for the four mages.

The projectiles strike true—and whistle clean through cloth and bone without meeting an ounce of resistance, plunging into the turf below.

"Illusions!" Arraka's shriek erupts from the amulet. "They were phantoms from the start!"

"Damn it, Arraka!" Daren shouts, drawing his sword as the clearing stirs. "You have the sharpest magical sense on the continent! How did you miss an illusion matrix?!"

"Shut up!" Arraka snarls. "Their auras felt solid down to the marrow! That green-robed lunatic must have pulled one of Memphir's master weaves and primed it before we entered the sector! It's the only explanation!"

"Guilty!" Clogworth crows from the grass. "Not even the legendary Arraka pierces a weave direct from the God of Fate! Just one of many revelations whispered into my dreams!"

"The wretch was born with an open channel to Memphir," Arraka tells us, voice dripping poison. "Instead of a broken link like Daren's, he got the unobstructed pipe. Argh! Why Memphir? His spell-knots are a nightmare to untangle!"

"Meaning you can't undo it," Illuna says.

"I can!" Arraka snaps. "It takes time! If you survive a hundred stillwaters while I work the knot, I'll hand you the counter!"

"A hundred what?!" Kate calls out.

The earth groans. Around our perimeter, a hundred bleached skeletons claw free of the soil, an aura of suffocating magnitude radiating from every skull.

"Shields up!" Daren plants his boots, snapping a white barrier over our heads. "Barry, how long for the device to draw a charge off this many signatures?"

"With a hundred of them? Under a minute."

"Reinforce me!" Daren shouts over the thunderous hammering of spells against the ward. "I need sixty seconds to get Barry's emergency boost online before we push out!"

"On it," Kate says, throwing both hands wide as a reinforced dome of ice encases Daren's white barrier.

I stand with the stat device gripped tight in my right fist, eyes glued to the display, waiting for the Emergency Mode prompt to flare to life. Seconds tick past. Nothing.

"Any progress, Barry?" Daren calls out.

"Nothing," I say. "The draw is dead. These skeletons are crafted tighter than Zack's—they barely bleed any ambient charge, even in a swarm."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Attack2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Attack2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Attack2
TEXT:

"Not what I wanted to hear," Daren grunts. "Will the emergency stats trigger, or do we break out?"

"At this bleed rate, the charge won't trip," I say. "How is the ward holding?"

"Solid," Daren says. "Despite the terrifying aura signature, their impacts carry almost no mass—"

"TRAPS!" Arraka screeches from the amulet. "All around your perimeter! Run! RUN!"

"Through the skeletons—" Daren shouts.

"THEY ARE SMOKE! PHANTOMS! RUN THROUGH THEM!"

"Left flank, move!" Daren points toward the pines. Kate drops the frost dome, and we surge forward, plunging straight through the wall of skeletal illusions.

The moment we clear the phantoms, an ear-splitting detonation tears the clearing apart behind us. Kate whips around mid-stride, throwing up a rear wall of ice to absorb the shockwave and flying dirt.

"Another one ahead!" Arraka yells.

A secondary charge detonates five paces in front of our boots. Daren throws up his shield just in time to catch the blast, but the concussive force hurls all of us backward onto the grass.

"ENOUGH!" Kate and Diane roar in unison.

They scramble to their feet, unleashing an instant wave of frost and crackling lightning across the clearing.

Five buried metallic canisters frost over and spark with blue electrical discharge, exposed by the blast radius. They lie scattered across the turf, planted in a hurry.

The remaining illusions dissolve. Only two skeletons are real. The four mages had been sheltering behind one of them, masked by Clogworth's weave. Kate's frost locks the first skeleton solid; the second raises a stone slab to deflect the elemental storm.

"You treacherous snake!" the lead necromancer snarls at Clogworth. "You were going to blow the girl to pieces in that blast!"

"Only after you announced to my face that you were going to betray me," Clogworth says, fingers flying across a small metallic console in his palm. "These explosives were a fallback for your inevitable incompetence. I should have detonated them the moment you engaged."

"Don't let them trigger the retreat, you fools!" Arraka shrieks. "Kill them! Kill them!"

"Retreat?" Hadrik blinks.

"Retreat?!" the necromancer yells, equally baffled.

"Quiet," Clogworth sneers, depressing the final rune on his console.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Attack3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Attack3)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Attack3
TEXT:

A brilliant column of white light envelops the four mages, the air vibrating with a high-pitched resonant hum.

"This isn't over," Clogworth calls out through the glare, locking eyes with us as Kate condenses a frost lance. "We will meet again. And next time you won't be so—"

"Lucky?" Flower offers as the four bodies dissolve into light and vanish.

Kate's frost projectile whistles through the empty space where Clogworth stood a fraction of a second earlier, embedding deep in the dirt.

"ARGH!" Arraka's fury rattles the metal of her casing. "Unbelievable! Another batch walks away! Talmak and his cronies, Tyrath's elite squads, Tyrath himself, Loyrang from the palace guard... am I missing anyone on your charity roster?!"

"Technically," I say, "Loyrang isn't really an enemy since he—"

"Shut it!" Arraka snarls. "I don't care about his life story! You are supposed to be hunting GODS! Act like it!"

"We're aiming at one god, last I checked," Daren says, poking at the scorched grass where the teleportation matrix fired. "And you asked for time to decipher the weave. If you want them pinned, work faster."

Arraka sputters in indignation, lacking a rebuttal for her own delay.

"Are there any more hidden explosives in the grass?" Illuna asks. "I would prefer not to detonate after the enemy has already fled."

"No," Arraka mutters, her tone cooling. "The lunatic placed them in a rush. I caught him stacking charges against Daren's ward while hidden by the phantasm, then he bolted and triggered them remotely."

"How far did they jump?" Kate asks.

"Beyond my sensory horizon," Arraka says. "Likely back to their main stronghold."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Attack4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Attack4)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Attack4
TEXT:

#if(v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt == 1) {
"Forget those cowards for a second," Diane says, hooking a casual arm around Kate's neck with a breathless laugh. "Did you feel that sync? We were practically twin sisters back there! We even have the scars to match!"

"Your scar is on your forearm," Kate says, her voice dry. "Mine is across my face."

"Details!" Diane beams. "Twin sisters in spirit!"

"Someone tell my sister to loosen her grip," Kate mutters. "Airflow is restricted."

"Calling for help already?" Diane's smirk widens, tightening the headlock just enough to tease. "And here I was, giving you the rematch you always wanted after all those times I pinned you at the Beacon!"

"Fine," Kate says. "You asked for this."

Kate sweeps her right heel behind Diane's lead ankle, locks both hands onto the grappling forearm, rotates her hips, and throws Diane cleanly over her shoulder onto the grass. Before Diane can roll, Kate drops a knee into her back and locks her wrist behind her shoulder blades.

}
#if(v_ch3_kate_badly_hurt != 1) {
"Forget those cowards for a second," Diane says, hooking a casual arm around Kate's neck with a breathless laugh. "Did you feel that sync? We were practically twin sisters back there!"

"Sisters, maybe," Kate says. "Twins? We look nothing alike."

"Twin sisters in spirit, Kate! In spirit!"

"Someone tell my sister to loosen her grip," Kate mutters. "Airflow is restricted."

"Calling for help already?" Diane's smirk widens, tightening the headlock just enough to tease. "And here I was, giving you the rematch you always wanted after all those times I pinned you at the Beacon!"

"Fine," Kate says. "You asked for this."

Kate sweeps her right heel behind Diane's lead ankle, locks both hands onto the grappling forearm, rotates her hips, and throws Diane cleanly over her shoulder onto the grass. Before Diane can roll, Kate drops a knee into her back and locks her wrist behind her shoulder blades.

}
"Concede?" Kate asks, applying steady pressure.

"Ow, ow, ow, ow!" Diane yelps.

"Concede?" Kate repeats, louder.

"Yield! I yield! You win!"

The moment Kate releases the wrist, Diane rolls onto her back and bursts into helpless, unrestrained laughter. There is no calculation in the sound, no guarded deflection. For the first time since she stepped out of the woods, the rigid mask drops, showing the girl who survived the Beacon.

Kate watches her for a beat, a genuine smile touching the corner of her mouth, and extends an open hand.

"Come on," Kate says. "Up you get."

"I can't believe you actually threw me!" Diane grabs Kate's forearm, wiping moisture from her eyes. "I wasn't expecting that at all!"

"You're the one who demanded a rematch," Kate says, hauling her upright.

"I was teasing!" Diane says, dusting off her tunic. "The old Kate from the Beacon would have hidden behind frost before she touched anyone. You were so shy back then! I think I like this version of you."

choice(""If you liked that throw, wait till you feel one of her punches!"", B3-Ch12b-Gadgets, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Gadgets, v_b3_ch12_punches = 0)
choice(""Kate being shy? Are you sure you're talking about the same person?"", B3-Ch12b-Gadgets, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Gadgets, v_b3_ch12_punches = 1)
choice(""I don't understand how you thought you could beat Kate in hand-to-hand combat."", B3-Ch12b-Gadgets, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Gadgets, v_b3_ch12_punches = 2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Gadgets
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_punches == 0) {
"If you liked that throw, wait till you feel one of her punches!"

"Glad to know you remember them so fondly, Barry," Kate says, cracking her knuckles with slow, deliberate precision. "Step closer. I have plenty in reserve."

"Generous, but I'll pass for now," I say, taking half a step back onto the gravel.

"No expiration date," Kate says. "Say something stupid, and you can claim your prize on the spot."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_punches == 1) {
"Kate being shy? Are you sure you're talking about the same person?"

"Oh, right," Diane says, shaking her head with a laugh. "The Kate you saw in the God of Time's trial was her adult mind driving a child's body. You never saw her before the world forced her to grow up. She was the sweetest, most timid girl in the entire compound!"

"Can we not dissect my childhood in public?" Kate mutters, looking away, her cheeks flushing slightly. "It's embarrassing."

"Look at that—you can still blush behind the cold stare!" Diane laughs warmly. "I like this Kate more and more."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_punches == 2) {
"I don't understand how you thought you could beat Kate in hand-to-hand combat."

"Aye, no joke," Hadrik says. "She tossed me during a sparring match once! Held back most of my weight, sure, but she used my own momentum to dump me square on my backside. Incredible leverage."

"It's a redirection discipline," Daren says, still pacing the burnt perimeter where the mages vanished. "Nolderan drilled me on the basics years ago. Uses an opponent's force against them. Didn't Kate mention an old martial master who took pity on her in the woods when her frost magic was still raw?"

"Right," Hadrik nods. "I remember that."

"You trained under an old master?" Diane asks, wide-eyed. "I'd love to meet him!"

"Not possible," Kate says quietly, looking down at her boots. "He passed away from old age."

"I'm sorry, Kate," Diane says, her tone softening with gentle sympathy.

}
"Can we hold this reunion on the road?" Arraka groans from the amulet. "The enemies fled five minutes ago. Why are we still standing around in the dirt?"

"I'm looking for the anchor," Daren says, kneeling near a patch of disturbed sod. "The illusionist triggered a handheld relay, which means the actual teleportation matrix remains buried somewhere in this clearing. We might salvage it."

"Why would he leave a functional core behind?" Arraka scoffs. "It's a disposable market gadget. Even I know the design, and I despise mortal trinkets. You're wasting daylight."

"A spent single-use teleporter can be rewired into an aura amplifier for twenty-four hours," Daren replies, his fingers sifting through the soil. "A trick from my early apprentice days when supplies ran lean—"

"Ugh," Arraka groans louder. "Even if you want the scrap, the green lunatic wrapped the core in a lingering concealment weave. It survived his departure, which means it's one of Memphir's nastier knots. I'm not unraveling another layer for a toy, and you can't see through it alone. Your only option is digging up every square yard of turf with your bare hands. Feel like spending the afternoon with a shovel, Daren?"

"Point taken," Daren sighs, standing up and brushing the loam from his knees. "We move."

"Music to my ears," Arraka purrs.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Digging, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Digging) if (v_b3_ch12_scout == 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Detector, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Detector) if (v_b3_ch12_scout != 3)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Digging
TEXT:

"Hold on," I say. "Remember the spot I flagged from the air? I watched the illusionist pack down soil right before we closed in. If I'm right, it's right about... here."

I walk past the charred circle to a patch of untouched grass near the tree line and drop to one knee, digging into the turf with both hands.

The topsoil yields instantly, loose and recently turned. After tossing aside a few handfuls of dirt, the concealing weave fractures with a faint pop. The phantom grass dissolves, revealing dark loam packed over a solid metallic frame.

Daren drops beside me, scooping away the remaining dirt until we unearth a bronze sphere roughly the size of a melon, etched with intricate conduit channels. Daren's eyes light up.

"Spot on," Daren says, pulling a leather roll of enchanting picks and chisels from his pack. "Give me sixty seconds to bridge the flow into an aura amplifier."

"How does one draw from it?" Illuna asks, watching him work. "Does it require physical contact during casting?"

"Proximity is enough," Daren says, his hands moving with practiced speed over the bronze casing. "It latches onto ambient aura and stabilizes the feed. Test it yourself in a second..."

With a final twist of his pick, Daren gives the sphere a light kick, rolling it across the grass to Illuna's feet.

Illuna nods, channeling the swirling water vortex she uses for morning cleaning drills. The sphere hums, its etched grooves glowing with a soft amber pulse. Illuna's eyes narrow in surprise.

"Better than anticipated," Illuna admits. "The vortex holds its geometry with far less strain. How long does the charge last?"

"A full day at most," Daren says, packing his tools. "Keep it close. If negotiations sour at the sacred woods, we might need every edge we have."

"Hadrik can haul it," Illuna says. "I have no desire to march with twenty pounds of bronze under my arm."

"I'll carry it," Hadrik grunts, picking up the sphere and stuffing it into his pack. "Though a 'please' or 'thank you' wouldn't crack your jaw once in a while."

"Please and thank you," Illuna says, flat as an iron plate.

"Progress," Hadrik chuckles.

"All set," Daren says, shouldering his gear. "Let's make ground."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Detector, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Detector)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Detector
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1) {
The two-hour march toward the sacred woods carries a taut, expectant silence. No mages cross our path, and Arraka detects no lingering signatures trailing our wake; the four assailants seem to have abandoned the hunt for now.

Between Kate and Diane, the brief wrestling match broke through years of estrangement. They walk in step, their easy back-and-forth carrying the unforced rhythm of their shared past at the Beacon of Hope, two survivors finally finding familiar ground beneath new scars.

The trail stays quiet aside from Sophie's regular check-in over the transceiver, Flower calling Melindra twice to debate strange cloud formations, and Hadrik loudly counting down the hours until he can wash the dust from his throat with ale.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 0) {
The two-hour march toward the sacred woods carries a taut, expectant silence. No mages cross our path, and Arraka detects no lingering signatures trailing our wake; the four assailants seem to have abandoned the hunt for now.

Between Kate and Diane, the brief wrestling match broke through years of estrangement. They walk in step, their easy back-and-forth carrying the unforced rhythm of their shared past at the Beacon of Hope, two survivors finally finding familiar ground beneath new scars.

The trail stays quiet aside from Sophie's regular check-in over the transceiver and Flower calling Melindra twice to debate strange cloud formations across the horizon.

}
By midday, the forest thins out until the trees give way to an immaculate boundary line. We pull up at the edge, the weight of the forest's guardian hanging over the quiet glade.

"Here we are," Daren says, breaking the stillness as he unhooks the metallic tablet from his belt. "Detector in hand. What's the plan?"

"I can sprint in, grab a cone, and sprint out," Hadrik offers. "No guards in sight. We could be ten miles out before anyone notices."

"Petal and I should do it," Flower says. "The fox never banned us from the sacred woods, and taking a single pinecone won't ruin the forest. No one will mind."

"Flower has the legal right of entry," Daren says. "Though splitting up still makes my neck itch."

"I'll search right along the border line," Flower says. "With any luck, there's one hanging five paces in and we never leave your sight."

"Fair enough," Daren says, looking around. "Everyone good with that?"

"Go for it," Hadrik says. "We hold the line right here."

"Take the detector," Kate says, passing the thin bronze tablet to Flower. "You remember the trigger?"

"Press the rune on top, right?" Flower takes the slab with a confident nod. "Easy. Wish me luck!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Pinecone, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Pinecone)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Pinecone
TEXT:

Flower steps past the tree line, treading softly over the bed of dry needles, her head on a swivel as if anticipating a pounce from the canopy. Beyond the perimeter, the sacred woods look identical to the mundane forest behind us, save for a faint amber shimmer filtering through the needles like liquid sunlight.

The golden hue is the only clue that this soil is forbidden ground, making the fox's lethal border policy feel all the more arbitrary.

"Found one!" Flower calls out, pointing up into the boughs.

The detector slab hums in her grip, and twenty paces in, a large pinecone blazes with vibrant golden radiance. Flower darts to the trunk, shimmies up the lower bark, and wrenches the glowing cone free. She hops down and hoists it high above her head as the light dims to a steady pulse.

"Got it!" Flower cheers. "Got it!"

A towering pillar of golden light erupts directly behind her shoulder, silent as falling snow. The brilliance folds inward, leaving the massive form of the golden fox standing over the girl.

Flower catches the displacement in her peripheral vision and locks up, her arms frozen mid-celebration.

choice(""Flower, watch out! There's a hideous monster right behind you!!!"", B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic, v_b3_ch12_hideous = 0)
choice(""We're not trespassing this time! Look, the border is over there!"", B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic, v_b3_ch12_hideous = 1)
choice("Say nothing.", B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic, v_b3_ch12_hideous = 2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Diplomatic
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_hideous == 0) {
"Flower, watch out! There's a hideous monster right behind you!!!"

"Where?!" Flower shrieks, whirling around with fists raised. "Where—oh. Oh, it's... good one, Barry. Very funny."

She lets out a short, high-pitched laugh that dies in her throat.

"What are you doing with the pinecone, Flower?" Eleya's voice resonates through the glade, deep and measured.

"This pinecone?" Flower looks down at her hand as if noticing it for the first time. "We need it for... well, there's this tournament, and—"

"I know the purpose of the pinecones," Eleya says. "Mages have trampled my borders for three days seeking them. What I asked is why you took property from my domain without permission."

"I... didn't think one would be missed," Flower murmurs, shrinking back.

"You thought wrong," the fox replies. "Hand it over."

"Could we... make a trade?" Flower asks. "We only need the one."

"A trade?" Eleya's golden eyes narrow.

"Yes," Flower says. "Name your terms."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_hideous == 1) {
"We're not trespassing this time! Look, the border is over there!"

"I never accused you of trespassing," Eleya says, her gaze resting impassively on me.

"Good," I say. "Just establishing baseline facts."

"So... am I free to go?" Flower asks, holding the cone behind her back.

"Of course," Eleya says. "The moment you return the pinecone."

"But we need it for—"

"I know what it is for," Eleya cuts her off. "Mages have trampled my borders for three days seeking them. Hand it over."

"Could we make a trade?" Flower asks. "Just one pinecone."

"A trade?" Eleya's golden eyes narrow.

"Yes," Flower says. "Name your terms."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_hideous == 2) {
"What are you doing with the pinecone, Flower?" Eleya breaks the silence, her voice rolling through the glade like distant thunder.

"This pinecone?" Flower looks down at her hand as if noticing it for the first time. "We need it for... well, there's this tournament, and—"

"I know the purpose of the pinecones," Eleya says. "Mages have trampled my borders for three days seeking them. What I asked is why you took property from my domain without permission."

"I... didn't think one would be missed," Flower murmurs, shrinking back.

"You thought wrong," the fox replies. "Hand it over."

"Could we... make a trade?" Flower asks. "We only need the one."

"A trade?" Eleya's golden eyes narrow.

"Yes," Flower says. "Name your terms."

}
"Looking at your company, you have grown considerably stronger since our last encounter," Eleya says, her gaze sweeping our line. "There is a task you can perform for me in exchange for the token."

"Name it!" Flower says instantly.

"A diplomatic summit convenes in Galamir shortly to coordinate defenses against Tyrath's forces," the fox says. "My lieutenant, Leo, is tasked with representing the forest. However, because foreign mages continuously invade my borders for tournament relics, I cannot spare veteran guardians to escort him along the public highways."

"Can't you monitor the perimeter and teleport when alarms trip, like you just did?" Flower asks.

"My awareness has limits, and my territory spans the continent," Eleya says. "My sentries must hold their posts. You will escort Leo to Galamir. As I understand, your second tournament objective directs you to the same city. The escort does not disrupt your itinerary. Do you accept?"

"Uh, yes... I think so," Flower glances back at us, catching our collective nods. "But... are you sure about sending Leo with humans? Wouldn't he travel safer alone than in the company of mages?"

"Flower," Eleya says gently. "For twenty years, you have tended the beasts of these woods without once demanding tribute. And as difficult as it is to acknowledge, you were correct about Illuna. She has proven a steadfast ally. I have confirmed Arraka engineered the tragedy of the sacred grove, and while forgiveness remains difficult, Illuna's actions carry context."

"Hmph," Illuna's voice issues from Flower's lips.

"Therefore," Eleya concludes, "your pledge to see Leo safely to Galamir is all the guarantee I require."

"Thank you, Eleya," Flower says, placing a hand over her chest. "I give you my word. We will bring him to Galamir safely."

"Very well," the fox says. "Wait here while I retrieve him."

The golden light folds around her, and she vanishes.

"Well," Hadrik mutters, leaning on his axe. "The fox has a soft underbelly after all. Who knew?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Wolf, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Wolf) if (v_b2_ch9_animals == 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Dwellers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Dwellers) if (v_b2_ch9_animals != 2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Wolf
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch12_languages,0) if (v_ancient_languages < 2)
set(v_b3_ch12_languages,1) if (v_ancient_languages >= 2)
 

We wait by the border markers as instructed. A few minutes later, golden radiance pulses in the clearing, depositing Leo the leopard onto the turf.

Beside him sits a gray wolf, a carved band of polished hardwood held carefully in his jaws. I recognize him after a second: one of the wolves we pulled from the blazing forest during the storm, accompanied by their bear protector. The wolf places the wooden band onto the grass before me.

"This beast insists you delivered him from a wildfire," Eleya says. "Is this accurate?"

"During the thunderstorm," I nod. "I remember."

"Then I leave you to your exchange," the fox says. "Leo, should necessity arise, you know the contact weave."

"Understood, Great Golden Fox," Leo dips his head. "I will manage."

#if(v_ancient_languages < 2) {
The golden light claims Eleya. The wolf lets out a low sequence of growls and chuffs, tilting his muzzle toward me. I catch the cadence of beast tongue, but with my current stat allocation in Ancient Languages, the grammar blurs into animal noise.

I try responding in the Common tongue, but the wolf only tilts his ears, confused. Leo steps up to bridge the gap.

"When I heard the Great Fox arranging an escort with two-legs, I knew the scent was yours," the wolf says through Leo's calm translation. "I pledged a boon should you ever return to the sacred boundaries. Take this band. It is my clan's token. Wear it with strength."

"Thank you," I say in Common, and Leo repeats the words in low growls.

I stoop down, slip the wooden band over my left wrist, and test the fit.

"Is there an activation phrase?" I ask. "Or a trigger condition?"

}
#if(v_ancient_languages >= 2) {
The golden light claims Eleya. The wolf steps forward and speaks in the guttural, melodic dialect of the elder predators.

"When I heard the Great Fox arranging an escort with two-legs, I knew the scent was yours," the wolf says, his tail swaying once. "I pledged a boon should you ever return to the sacred boundaries. Take this band. It is my clan's token. Wear it with strength."

"Thank you," I say, dipping my head.

I pick up the wooden band from the turf and slide it onto my left wrist.

"Is there an activation phrase?" I ask. "Or a trigger condition?"

}
"Human enchantments have no parallel for this craft," the wolf explains. "The weave functions best when the bearer remains ignorant of its mechanics. Consider it a talisman against despair, designed to tip the scales against an adversary of overwhelming force. It activates once, then the wood returns to ash. Do not rely on it twice."

"Good to know," I say. "I will keep it on my wrist. Safe travels, friend."

"May the wind favor your path, human," the wolf replies.

"Shall we make ground?" Diane asks, adjusting her cloak.

"The highway to Galamir is familiar?" Leo asks.

"Main road follows the valley signs," Diane says. "Direct route."

"Lead on," Leo says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Dwellers, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Dwellers)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Dwellers
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch9_animals != 2) {
We wait by the border markers as instructed. A few minutes later, golden radiance pulses in the clearing, depositing Leo the leopard onto the turf.

"Introductions are unnecessary since you've crossed paths before," Eleya says before anyone can speak. "Leo, should necessity arise, you know the contact weave."

"Understood, Great Golden Fox," Leo dips his head. "I will manage."

"Shall we make ground?" Diane asks as the golden fox dissolves into light.

"The highway to Galamir is familiar?" Leo asks.

"Main road follows the valley signs," Diane says. "Direct route."

"Lead on," Leo says.

"I am sorry for dragging you into this, Leo," Flower says quietly. "I know how you feel about humans..."

"No apology is required," Leo replies, falling into stride beside her. "The Great Fox assigned this escort, not you. Furthermore, my contempt is reserved for the lords and butchers of the stone cities, not for your companions."

}
#if(v_b2_ch9_animals == 2) {
"I am sorry for dragging you into this, Leo," Flower says quietly. "I know how you feel about humans..."

"No apology is required," Leo replies, falling into stride beside her. "The Great Fox assigned this escort, not you. Furthermore, my contempt is reserved for the lords and butchers of the stone cities, not for your companions."

}
"That makes me feel better!" Flower brightens. "Welcome to the team, Leo!"

"My thanks," Leo says gravely.

"We actually have the glowing pinecone in our possession," Hadrik marvels, ignoring the cat. "An actual tournament objective, finished ahead of time!"

"Technically we have to deliver it, but yes," Daren says with a rare grin.

"I wonder if we're the first squad to reach Galamir with the prize!" Hadrik chuckles.

"Stop advertising our cargo to the countryside," Illuna snaps. "Carrying a beacon of light through contested territory is foolish enough without shouting it to every bush."

"Right, right," Hadrik grunts. "Wouldn't want any unexpected brawls. Heaven forbid."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Eiden, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Eiden)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Eiden
TEXT:

We leave the sacred glade behind and strike out for the paved highway. The road to Galamir cuts through rolling pine ridges, deserted and quiet beneath the high sun, the nearby presence of the sacred woods keeping merchants and travelers away from the route. Flower, Kate, and I try drawing Leo into conversation, but Daren and Hadrik keep a cold, measured distance from the former jailer.

Given our history with the animal kingdom, their caution is understandable, though Leo did open the gate to our trial when the fox wanted executions. He earned a clean slate in my book.

A bend in the highway opens onto a long straight stretch of cobblestone.

Standing dead center in the middle of the road is Eiden, floating an inch above the dust, hands resting in his pockets, his eyes shut and that familiar, placid smile fixed on his lips.

"Good day, my friends," Eiden says as our boots slow on the stone. "I see you've recruited new company. How fares the forest, Leo? It has been some time."

"It has," Leo murmurs, his ears pinning back in discomfort.

"And you carry a glowing pinecone as well," Eiden continues, tilting his head toward the pack. "Splendid work. The organizers will be thrilled."

"Is there a purpose to this visit, Eiden?" Daren asks, stepping forward.

"A few," Eiden says lightly. "First, I was curious whether you have given further thought to Meridith's proposal regarding my assassination. Two days have passed. Surely the topic crossed your campfire once or twice?"

choice("Tell Eiden that we've tried to avoid this subject as much as possible.", B3-Ch12b-Eiden2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Eiden2, v_b3_ch12_hesitant = 0)
choice("Tell Eiden about Daren being hesitant to kill him.", B3-Ch12b-Eiden2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Eiden2, v_b3_ch12_hesitant = 1)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Eiden2
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_hesitant == 0) {
"Actually, we've tried to avoid this subject as much as possible," I say.

"Mhm... sensible," Eiden nods. "Stall the decision, avoid declaring me an enemy, yet keep the door ajar for Meridith should the mood strike. Very prudent."

Silence settles over the highway.

"Was there anything else," Hadrik grunts, "or did you just stop by to admire our scenery?"

"One small matter," Eiden says, his smile widening slightly. "A peculiar puzzle over the past two days. Every surface lessathi I've tracked since the tournament opened abruptly vanished from my sensory field, shortly after you entered their compound. Not a single trace since. Any thoughts on why that might be?"

Sweat runs cold down my neck.

"No idea," Hadrik says cheerfully. "Maybe Meridith recalled them underground?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_hesitant == 1) {
"Actually, I spoke with Daren last night," I say. "Recent events shifted his perspective. He thinks you haven't lost all of your humanity, and he'd hesitate to take Meridith's bounty."

"Is that so?" Eiden's closed face turns toward Daren, thoughtful.

"Was there anything else," Hadrik grunts, "or did you just stop by to admire our scenery?"

"One small matter," Eiden says, his smile widening slightly. "A peculiar puzzle over the past two days. Every surface lessathi I've tracked since the tournament opened abruptly vanished from my sensory field, shortly after you entered their compound. Not a single trace since. Any thoughts on why that might be?"

Sweat runs cold down my neck.

"No idea," Hadrik says cheerfully. "Maybe Meridith recalled them underground?"

}
"My initial thought as well," Eiden says. "A quiet retreat seemed reasonable. But when none surfaced this morning, curiosity got the better of me. I spent a few hours flying invisibly over the valleys, scanning the terrain. And do you know what I spotted?"

"...A lessathi?" Flower asks.

"A lessathi! Exactly, Flower. But strangely, I couldn't feel his presence until I was practically breathing on his neck. I examined the concealing talisman tucked into his sleeve, and what do you know—upgraded a hundredfold. A hundred times its previous dampening strength. You wouldn't happen to know what prompted such an upgrade, would you, Barry?"

"It was a mistake!" The words tear out of my throat. "I didn't know it was confidential! I let it slip to Meridith that you could track them from across the continent! Read my aura—I'm telling the truth!"

"I know you are," Eiden purrs. "And as mature individuals, what matters is acknowledging our blunders, learning the lesson, and ensuring they are never repeated. Isn't that right, Barry?"

"Uh... yes. Exactly."

"Splendid," Eiden says cheerfully. "Then we are in accord. Diane must die."

"What?!" Kate's shout shatters the quiet, the rest of us frozen in disbelief.

"Come now, Kate," Eiden sighs. "Diane is Meridith's second-in-command. Every word you traded with her on the road will be delivered to Meridith the moment she returns underground. Whatever you revealed about me over the past two days becomes intelligence for my enemies. I cannot permit that."

"What else could we possibly have told her that matters?" I demand.

"I have no idea," Eiden shrugs. "I never anticipated you would leak my sensory range, so there may well be other crucial details you disclosed during our frequent chats. Since I refuse to memorize every syllable we've exchanged, and you certainly cannot recount every campfire discussion with Diane, removing Diane from the board is the cleanest solution. Wouldn't you agree?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Eiden3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Eiden3)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Eiden3
TEXT:

"I am not here on an espionage mission," Diane says, taking a step forward. "I don't recall anything of value, and even if I did, I won't report it to Meridith. I only wanted to walk with my friend to Galamir."

"A bold claim from behind a fully masked aura," Eiden replies. "You know I cannot verify your sincerity. And even if your intent is pure now, loyalties shift once you reach the safety of your tunnels. You leave me no alternative."

"Don't you dare touch Diane," Kate steps in front of her, the scepter leveled straight at Eiden's chest. "Don't you dare."

"Kate, you fail to grasp how severely Barry's leak complicates my affairs," Eiden says, his tone hardening. "I am being remarkably generous. Under normal circumstances, I would erase your entire company where you stand. I made a promise to Barry in exchange for his delivery, which is why I offer this concession. Diane is second-in-command of a hostile faction whose leader wants me dead. She knew the risks when she surfaced. Do you truly wish to throw your lives away on her behalf?"

"Take your generous offer and shove it," Hadrik steps shoulder-to-shoulder with Kate, raising his fists. "You don't lay a finger on Diane while I draw breath."

"I fail to see how you intend to stop me," Eiden tilts his head, genuinely puzzled.

"Stand down, Eiden," Daren says, his hand on his pommel. "Don't do something you cannot undo."

"Perhaps I have indulged this group too much," Eiden murmurs. "You seem to have forgotten who stands before you. A brief reminder."

A crushing downward force hits like a dropped portcullis. My knees smash into the stone, air driven from my lungs as invisible gravity pins Daren, Hadrik, Flower, and me flat against the road. Only Kate, Diane, and Leo remain upright.

"Ngh..." Daren groans, muscles straining against the pavement. "Eiden, stop!"

"Since Diane means so much to you, Kate, I offer you the chance to save her," Eiden says, ignoring Daren.

Diane gasps, both hands flying to her throat as she is lifted three feet off the ground, thrashing against an invisible noose.

"Break my hold before her air runs out, and I might reconsider her execution," Eiden says. "Show me what your training produced."

"Monster!" Kate screams.

She unleashes a volley of jagged frost spears from her scepter, driving them straight at Eiden's head.

A translucent barrier flares in front of Eiden, shattering the ice into powder without budging an inch as he continues the choke.

"Eiden, stop!" Daren roars against the stone.

Kate adjusts instantly. Instead of targeting Eiden, she directs a burst of needle-thin icicles into the space between Eiden and Diane, slicing through the invisible aura conduit.

"You're not even aiming at me," Eiden remarks, oblivious to the tactic. "Your projectiles are flying wide."

Diane's eyes roll back, her body going limp. In that final fraction of a second, Kate condenses a massive frost wedge and drives it cleanly through the nexus of the aura tether.

The invisible grip snaps. Diane drops onto the cobblestones. Kate dives to her side, checking the pulse at her throat.

"She's breathing," Kate gasps. "She's alive."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Eiden4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Eiden4)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Eiden4
TEXT:

"How did you..." Eiden murmurs, his closed eyes fixed on Kate. "How did you sever that link?"

"I said KNOCK IT OFF!" Daren roars.

He sweeps his sword in a wide arc, the blade's edge shearing through the gravity field like silk, releasing the pressure instantly. We scramble to our feet.

"Aura disruption..." Eiden's smile returns, sharp and intrigued. "So that is how you dismantled the Ollendor captain. Outstanding. Perhaps I should test you at a third of my strength? No... a fifth? A tenth, perhaps?"

"You love the sound of your own voice, don't you?" Hadrik grins, exploding off the stone in a blur.

His fist drives forward, punching a crater into the cobblestone as Eiden drifts backward effortlessly.

"Resuming our tournament duel, dwarf?" Eiden asks. "Shall I abstain from magic once more to keep it sporting?"

"Suit yourself," Hadrik snarls, pressing forward with a relentless flurry of hooks and jabs.

For ten seconds, Eiden bobs and weaves with effortless grace. But as Hadrik accelerates, the rhythm shifts. Hadrik drives inside Eiden's guard, cutting off his angles, forcing him into a defensive crouch.

A heavy overhand right breaks through. Eiden throws up a barrier at the last millisecond, the concussive force hurling him ten paces back across the road.

"What happened to no magic?" Hadrik laughs, shaking out his knuckles. "Or was that shimmering blue wall made of fresh air?"

Eiden's smile tightens into a frown.

"Reading my aura to dodge only works when I plan my strikes," Hadrik says. "Daren's been pulling that trick in sparring for days. Strike on raw instinct without a picture in your skull, and the aura scrambles into noise. Your little cheat is useless."

"Eiden, end this," Daren says, stepping forward. "You are losing your reason. This isn't you."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Eiden5, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Eiden5)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Eiden5
TEXT:

"Interrupting a duel is poor manners, healer," Eiden says, flicking two fingers toward Daren. "Sleep."

Nothing happens. Eiden flicks his fingers twice more, his brow knitting before the realization lands.

"Ah... the protection overload," Eiden muses. "Barry diagnosed it, and you cleared the loop."

"I fixed a great deal more than the overload," Daren says, leveling his sword in the low, fluid stance of Nolderan's discipline. "If you want a demonstration, take it."

Daren closes the distance, blade sweeping in a lethal pattern.

From inside the amulet, Arraka mutters in rising panic: "No, no, no... It's too early. If he gets serious, we are finished. He will ruin everything!"

She is right. If Eiden drops the performance and channels the devastating magnitude he unleashed against Tyrath in the Thilias arena, we die in seconds. Our only hope is defusing this before he unleashes full power, or striking him down while he is still toying with us.

Part of me still rebels against the reality. We spent weeks navigating around this monster, hoping to reach the finale without a direct confrontation. Maybe there is still a phrase, an offer, a compromise that stops the slaughter.

choice("Try to de-escalate the conflict.", B3-Ch12b-Deescalate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Deescalate)
choice("Try to find a way to defeat Eiden before he unleashes his full power.", B3-Ch12b-Unleashes, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Unleashes)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Deescalate
TEXT:

"Eiden, wait!" I call out as Daren drives him back. "What if Diane takes an enchanter's oath? A blood-bound vow never to disclose a single word about you to Meridith or the lessathi? That solves the problem!"

"An enchanter's oath..." Eiden deflects Daren's blade with a flick of his wrist.

"Yes! Total confidentiality!" My pulse thumps in my ears. "We put this behind us and keep our arrangement intact!"

Eiden vanishes.

He reappears an inch from my nose, eyes closed, his smile completely extinguished.

"You disappoint me, Barry," Eiden says, his voice cold as iron. "Your companions recognized immediately that negotiation was dead, and they bet their lives on the fraction of a chance they could fight. And what do you do? Cower behind paperwork? Cling to the childish delusion that empty promises to save cities grant you immunity? What was the point of pursuing magic if you cringe the moment death looks at you? Were you simply a coward all along?"

Every instinct in my gut screams that this is execution.

I trigger my air currents, rocketing upward to bring my hands to bear.

Eiden is gone.

I whip around in mid-air, scanning the sky. Above? Below? Where—

"Invisible, you fool!" Arraka screams. "Behind you!"

A hand locks around the back of my skull. Concussive force detonates inside my brain, shattering bone and consciousness into nothingness.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Unleashes
TEXT:

Negotiation is dead. Eiden will not stop until Diane is dead, and the moment we step between them, the choice narrows to kill or be killed.

Daren steps inside Eiden's guard, blade slashing horizontally. Eiden raises his left hand and catches the enchanted edge dead center on the pad of his index finger.

Focal hardening. Eiden concentrates the entire density of his aura into a needle-thin contact point.

Daren doesn't blink. He twists his wrists, sending the enchanted edge into a high-speed spin against the fingertip. Eiden blinks away in a displacement flash, reappearing ten paces back—wary of the cut that severs mana links.

"That sword is becoming a nuisance," Eiden says, extending an open palm to yank the weapon from Daren's grip.

"Take it!" Daren lets go.

The blade surges toward Eiden, accelerating into a whistling blur like a turbine rotor. Eiden teleports ten paces right; the blade banks instantly in mid-air, tracking his displacement. Eiden blasts a localized gale at the weapon; the enchanted steel drinks the air element and hurls a compressed shockwave right back at his face.

Eiden displaces again, a decoy silhouette lingering while his true signature slips into invisibility.

"Phantasm!" Arraka roars from the amulet. "The real one is behind you!"

Hadrik pivots on his heel, driving a massive haymaker into empty air. His knuckles connect with solid flesh, shattering the invisibility weave and sending Eiden crashing through a pine trunk by the roadside.

"Assisting them, Arraka?" Eiden asks, rising from the splinters and dusting off his shoulders. "Hardly sportsmanlike."

"Shut your filthy mouth!" Arraka shrieks. "You are going to ruin five thousand years of planning! Five thousand years!"

"I haven't the faintest idea what you mean," Eiden says as Hadrik barrels through the broken trunk, driving another heavy fist into his ribs.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Unleashes2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Unleashes2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Unleashes2
TEXT:

I need to read the board with cold precision. Right now, Daren and Hadrik carry the front line, but Eiden is only indulging them. Even restrained, his reserves dwarf anything we can match in a battle of attrition.

Our only path to survival is executing the anti-Arraka sequence we drilled for the past duplicate. It requires pinning Eiden motionless and blinding him for three full seconds so Kate can channel the scepter's absolute freeze beam.

To set up that window, Eiden needs to be off-balance and disoriented before I spring the trap.

And for that, I need pressure from above.

"Flower, Illuna," I call out, tracking the clash in the dust. "We need to box him in. Ready?"

"You have a sequence in mind?" Illuna's voice answers.

"I do, but I'm keeping it off the air in case his sensory amplification is tracking our speech," I say.

"Understood," Illuna replies. "Signal when you move."

I nod and turn to Kate, who remains stationed over Diane's still form.

"Kate," I say quietly. "The anti-Arraka drill."

"I remember," Kate says, her knuckles tight on the scepter.

"Conserve every scrap of mana," I tell her. "Do not commit until I trigger the opening. You'll know the signal."

"I'll be ready," Kate says.

choice("Offer support to Flower and Illuna, but try to keep a distance from Eiden.", B3-Ch12b-Support, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Support)
choice("Attack Eiden together with Flower and Illuna.", B3-Ch12b-Together, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Together)
choice("Go all out (Requires max Strength, Speed, Reflexes, Toughness, Aura hardening)", B3-Ch12b-Allout1, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Allout1, v_b3_ch12_eiden = 4) if (v_agility == 4 && v_aura_hardening == 4 && v_reflexes == 4 && v_strength == 4 && v_toughness == 4)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Support
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch11_red == 0 && v_b3_ch12_scout == 0) {
"Move," I tell Illuna.

Flower launches into the air, twin fire jets blasting from her boots, while I channel my air currents to lift off behind her.

The currents wobble under my ribs, fighting for balance, but with one point in magical stats I take what I can get.

Ahead of us, Daren and Hadrik have turned the highway into a disaster zone. Half the pines along the verge lie splintered or shorn by Daren's floating steel, and Eiden drifts through the wreckage with effortless grace, barely tapping his deeper reserves.

When Flower draws near, Illuna conjures three water lances and hurls them downward. Eiden raises his right palm, flash-boiling the projectiles into steam with a torrent of white flame.

"More guests?" Eiden smiles, raising an eyebrow. "Shall we move to a fifth, then?"

"A fifth?" Hadrik roars. "You arrogant little—"

A wave of flame washes across the cobblestone. Daren locks his shield in front of Hadrik, while Illuna drops a roaring pillar of water to shield our climb.

}
#if(v_b3_ch11_red == 1 && v_b3_ch12_scout == 0) {
"Move," I tell Illuna.

Flower launches into the air, twin fire jets blasting from her boots, while I channel my air currents to lift off behind her.

The node's principles hold my flight steady, the currents responding smoothly to every shift in weight despite my single stat point.

Ahead of us, Daren and Hadrik have turned the highway into a disaster zone. Half the pines along the verge lie splintered or shorn by Daren's floating steel, and Eiden drifts through the wreckage with effortless grace, barely tapping his deeper reserves.

When Flower draws near, Illuna conjures three water lances and hurls them downward. Eiden raises his right palm, flash-boiling the projectiles into steam with a torrent of white flame.

"More guests?" Eiden smiles, raising an eyebrow. "Shall we move to a fifth, then?"

"A fifth?" Hadrik roars. "You arrogant little—"

A wave of flame washes across the cobblestone. Daren locks his shield in front of Hadrik, while Illuna drops a roaring pillar of water to shield our climb.

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_scout != 0) {
"Move," I tell Illuna.

Flower launches into the air, twin fire jets blasting from her boots, while I channel my air currents to lift off behind her.

Ahead of us, Daren and Hadrik have turned the highway into a disaster zone. Half the pines along the verge lie splintered or shorn by Daren's floating steel, and Eiden drifts through the wreckage with effortless grace, barely tapping his deeper reserves.

When Flower draws near, Illuna conjures three water lances and hurls them downward. Eiden raises his right palm, flash-boiling the projectiles into steam with a torrent of white flame.

"More guests?" Eiden smiles, raising an eyebrow. "Shall we move to a fifth, then?"

"A fifth?" Hadrik roars. "You arrogant little—"

A wave of flame washes across the cobblestone. Daren locks his shield in front of Hadrik, while Illuna drops a roaring pillar of water to shield our climb.

}
"I'll provide long-range artillery," I shout to Illuna, banking wide to open distance. "I have spells that can—"

"Support from afar?" Eiden's voice whispers directly in my ear. He floats beside my shoulder, keeping pace in mid-air. "You delay entering the fray for minutes, only to retreat to the perimeter? When did you turn into such a coward, Barry?"

I whip my hand around, snapping a bolt of lightning at his face. Eiden vanishes before the arc connects.

I pivot in mid-air, flooding the empty sky behind me with roaring flames to catch an invisible silhouette, but the heat finds nothing.

A hand clamps onto the crown of my head from above. Concussive force rips through bone and brain, terminating consciousness in a blinding flash.

