ID: B3-Ch12b-Blinding
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch12_dagger,1) if (v_b3_ch12_eiden == 1)
set(v_ac_b3_ch12_delegator,1) if (v_b3_ch12_eiden == 2)
set(v_ac_b3_ch12_hugger,1) if (v_b3_ch12_eiden == 4)
set(v_ac_b3_ch12_liar,1) if (v_b3_ch12_approach == 2 && v_b3_ch12_eiden == 0 && v_b3_ch12_hadrik == 1)
achievement("Serial liar",v_ac_b3_ch12_liar)
achievement("Delegator",v_ac_b3_ch12_delegator)
achievement("Tree-hugger",v_ac_b3_ch12_hugger)
achievement("One Dagger Man",v_ac_b3_ch12_dagger)
I channel the dizziness matrix instantly. Just like the baseline test on Diane, a piercing harmonic screech floods his inner ear, the signature frequency of Wolford's daze.

In a calm state, Eiden would detect the minor variance in my cast. But battered by water, wind, and impact, facing an incantation that demands immediate sensory grounding before it paralyzes the motor cortex, Eiden has only one recourse.

He opens his eyes wide.

"Daren, catch!" I roar.

At the drilled code word, my companions shut their eyelids tight. I snap mine closed as well and detonate a blinding flash of incandescent white light across the glade.

For a mage who keeps his eyes shut for centuries, sudden optical overload shatters both physical vision and delicate magical sense in a single blinding rupture.

Kate needs no order. Her scepter is already leveled, its focus gem blazing with arctic fury.

"Now," Kate whispers.

A concentrated beam of absolute frost erupts from the scepter, hammering into Eiden's chest. The thermal drain is instantaneous. A monolithic block of reinforced, opaque ice encases his entire frame, locking him rigid in the center of the road.

The setup held. Every piece aligned.

Only the final cut remains.

I turn to Daren. He stands five paces from the ice block, sword raised in both hands, his knuckles white, breath rattling in his throat.

His boots don't move.

He is not paralyzed by fear—he is paralyzed by who he is. Daren is a healer and an idealist; for days he has clung to the conviction that the man who spared the slave girl in Thilias and said prove me wrong could still be pulled back from the dark. His enchanted blade can sever a mage's link to the magical plane with a shallow strike, stripping power without taking life. But through three feet of solid, opaque ice, a blow heavy enough to breach the frost will cleave the stillwater in two. To incapacitate without executing demands a butcher's calculation Daren cannot make in cold blood.

He hesitates, searching for an angle of mercy—and in that fraction of a second, the opportunity dies.

"Daren?" Flower's voice trembles, watching him frozen over the frost, sweat dripping from his chin. "Is everything all right?"

"What are you waiting for, you idiot?!" Arraka screams from the amulet. "You have the Creator of Varathia helpless in ice! Cut him down before he—"

"Too late."

The ice block shatters in a concussive shockwave.

The atmosphere plummets into suffocating density, the sheer weight of Eiden's unleashed aura crushing the breath from our lungs, identical to the cataclysmic presence he displayed against Tyrath in the Thilias arena.

"I indulged your games long enough," Eiden says, his voice flat as a frozen lake. "Time to end this."

choice("Taunt Eiden.", B3-Ch12b-Taunt, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Taunt)
choice("Stay silent.", B3-Ch12b-Invitation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Invitation)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Taunt
TEXT:

"End this?" I sneer. "You would have ended it ten minutes ago if you could. The scary aura act isn't fooling anyo—"

Eiden doesn't speak. He raises one index finger.

Concussive force detonates inside my skull, obliterating bone and brain in a microsecond.

choice("Restart game", Ch1-Intro1, v_current_scene = Ch1-Intro1, special:restart)
choice("Load from last checkpoint", , v_chapter_save_counter = 5, special:checkpoint_load)
choice("Load game", , , special:saves)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Invitation
TEXT:

"Leo, step back into the pines," Eiden says, glancing toward the leopard. "From this moment onward, I cannot guarantee your safety."

"Understood," Leo murmurs, ears pinned back as he shrinks into the deep shadows of the brush.

"Now, then," Eiden turns back to our battered line. "Who goes first?"

"Hah!" Hadrik grins, surging off the cobblestones. "The brawl I came for!"

Hadrik lunges with a crushing hook. Eiden doesn't shift his stance; his hand shoots out in a blur, catches Hadrik by the brow, and drives his skull straight into the pavement with sickening concussive weight. The dwarf goes limp instantly, his heavy frame slumping motionless in the dust.

Daren hurls his sword into a screaming spin. Eiden meets the flying steel with a concentrated torrent of white fire so dense that it overloads the weapon's elemental threshold in a heartbeat, detonating the matrix and blasting Daren backward into the roadside ditch. Daren crashes into the gorse, his tunic scorched and his limbs refusing to obey.

"LEAVE THEM ALONE!" Flower screams, her eyes blazing crimson, her aura igniting into raw, roaring flame.

She unleashes a towering pillar of fire directly at Eiden's chest, matching his own intensity with blinding fury. Eiden raises his palm, holding the inferno back with visible effort as the cobblestones beneath his boots melt into slag.

"Flower, you're burning your lifeforce!" Illuna's voice screams through their shared throat. "Stop!"

"I can't!" Flower gasps through the glare. "We're all that's left! I have to—"

Eiden displaces behind her, the side of his palm striking the base of her neck with surgical precision. The flames extinguish in an instant. Flower and Illuna collapse together onto the scorched turf, silent and unmoving.

Kate raises her scepter with shaking hands, but her reservoir is completely spent. The focus gem gutters out into dead silver, and her knees buckle hard against the stone.

The silence that drops over the highway is absolute, broken only by the crackle of burning brush. In less than twenty seconds, every frontline pillar that carried us across three books lies broken and motionless on the road. Hadrik bleeding into the dust; Flower and Illuna limp; Kate gasping against the cobblestones; Daren pinned in the ditch.

Daren claws his way upright on trembling forearms, spitting dark blood onto the gravel as Eiden's suffocating aura bears down like a falling mountain.

"Eiden..." Daren rasps, his chest heaving with agonizing effort. "This isn't you. You don't want this. Why—why do this?!"

#if(v_b3_ch12_hesitant == 1) {
"Seconds from execution, and you still plead," Eiden says, stepping closer with slow, measured strides, his boots clicking on the cooling stone. "Yesterday you told Barry you'd hesitate to take my life. How does a man shift perspective so completely in a handful of days? You, who charged me like a rabid dog at every crossroad? Did a shadow memory from the God of Time's trial soften your spine?"

"Because you told me to prove you wrong!" The words tear out of Daren's throat, raw, jagged, and broken. Eiden's closed eyelids flinch. "I know you don't remember it here, but it was you! In Thilias—the slave girl—I knelt in the dirt and begged you for the power to save her! You gave it to me! You looked me in the eye and told me to prove you wrong!"

Eiden halts, the pieces locking together behind his closed eyes. "So that is what occurred..."

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_hesitant == 0) {
"Seconds from execution, and you still plead," Eiden says, stepping closer with slow, measured strides, his boots clicking on the cooling stone. "How does a man shift perspective so completely in a handful of days? You, who charged me like a rabid dog at every crossroad? Did a shadow memory from the God of Time's trial soften your spine?"

"Because you told me to prove you wrong!" The words tear out of Daren's throat, raw, jagged, and broken. Eiden's closed eyelids flinch. "I know you don't remember it here, but it was you! In Thilias—the slave girl—I knelt in the dirt and begged you for the power to save her! You gave it to me! You looked me in the eye and told me to prove you wrong!"

Eiden halts, the pieces locking together behind his closed eyes. "So that is what occurred..."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Invitation2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Invitation2)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Invitation2
TEXT:

"You never spoke those words in this timeline, but you carry them!" Daren gasps, fighting for air beneath the crushing pressure. "Deep down, you want someone to prove you were wrong to give up on humanity! Give us the chance! We can make a difference!"

"Too late, Daren," Eiden says softly, every trace of warmth gone from his voice.

"Why?!" Daren's voice cracks with desperation. "Why is it too late?!"

"The blue crystals are awake," Eiden replies, the words dropping like lead weights.

Daren stares up from the dirt, uncomprehending. "What does that have to do with—"

"You think this island, this tournament, is the boundary of existence?" Eiden's voice drops to a lethal, chilling register. "If Meridith achieves her aim, it will unmake us all—this world and every realm tethered to it."

"We didn't know," Daren strains against the road. "If you would just explain—"

"No," Eiden says flatly. "Diane dies. You failed to stop me. Now you pay the price."

"EIDEN!"

Arraka's fury detonates from the amulet, the metal casing vibrating with terrifying, earth-shaking resonance.

"Touch them, and I will scour the infinite void of the ether until I unearth your sanctuary! I will tear your soul from its anchor and torture it for eternity! I will turn your existence into an endless waking nightmare!"

Eiden blinks, genuine confusion breaking his composure. "What has come over you? I am not harming Flower. My pledge to the fox remains intact."

"ANY OF THEM!" Arraka's voice shakes the trees along the road. "Kill ANY of them, and you will curse the day you drew breath! Five thousand years of waiting—five thousand years!—and I am finally alive! You will not rob me of my revenge against the gods!"

Eiden tilts his head, stunned. "For a moment, it sounded as though you claim to be training these mortals to slaughter gods. But surely that is absurd..."

"It is EXACTLY what I am doing, you arrogant parasite!"

Silence drops over the road. Eiden stands rigid, color draining from his face.

"You... are training them to kill gods?" Eiden whispers, the words catching in his throat. "These... clowns? You want them to slay gods?"

He looks at us, the absurdity warring with the sheer audacity of the claim.

"Look at them!" Eiden gestures to our broken line. "I could extinguish them with a thought!"

"They had you dead to rights, you fool!" Arraka roars. "If Daren hadn't stayed his blade for mercy, you would be in two pieces on this cobblestone! You think gods fight harder than a freeze beam through the throat? You have no concept of what these weaklings become under my hand! None!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Invitation3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Invitation3)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Invitation3
TEXT:

Eiden stands in the quiet, his aura settling into deadly focus. "Perhaps you are correct. Perhaps they could have killed me. All the more reason to eliminate them now, before the seeds take root. Farewell to your grand design, Arraka."

"NO!" Arraka shrieks from the talisman, the copper disc burning against Flower's collarbone.

I draw my dagger with numb fingers, scraping my boots against the scorched stone for a final, hopeless stand.

"I wouldn't advise that," a calm, resonant voice remarks from the roadside.

The air does not lighten; it grows instantly colder, the ambient mana thickening until my chest aches. A second presence steps out from the shadows of the pines—not a savior, but another apex predator stepping into the clearing.

Azarius stands ten paces away, hands folded neatly inside his voluminous gray robes, his weathered face entirely tranquil. His pale eyes fix on Eiden with the polite, unflinching detachment of a man examining an insect pinned to cork.

"I felt your scrying from the moment our duel began, Sage of the West," Eiden says, turning his head slowly toward the newcomer. "You are called wise, yet wisdom dictated staying hidden while I finished this business. What madness drove you to step into the open before my unleashed power? Do you truly believe you can defeat me?"

"Heavens, no," Azarius chuckles softly, the sound dry as parchment. "I wouldn't dream of attempting it."

"Then how do you propose to stop me?"

"I anticipated the question," Azarius says, and snaps his fingers.

A sharp resonant chime echoes across the ether. Far across the western horizon, a massive strand of magical tapestry unweaves and dissolves into nothingness. Eiden's closed eyes flinch, his head snapping toward the distant fracture.

"What did you..." Eiden whispers, his voice dropping into dangerous stillness.

"I discovered your continental detection grid weeks ago," Azarius says smoothly. "A marvel of engineering. Knowing its importance to your operations, I altered the anchor matrices in secret while dampening my signature. As you just witnessed, a single gesture unravels a node and voids hours of delicate calibration. Rebuilding the weave requires starting from scratch. And more importantly—should you strike me down, my death will trigger an immediate cascading collapse of every detection spell across Varathia."

Eiden's posture turns rigid, white-hot fury radiating from his frame with enough intensity to singe the pine needles under his boots.

"You dare blackmail me? You dare blackmail ME?"

"Naturally," Azarius smiles, serene and utterly remorseless. "Why wouldn't I?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Invitation4, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Invitation4)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Invitation4
TEXT:

"You understand that the moment these spells have served their purpose, I will hunt you across the world and slaughter you?" Eiden's voice is barely above a hiss.

"By all means, try," Azarius says cheerfully. "Though I suspect you will find yourself somewhat indisposed when the time arrives."

"What does that mean?"

"You will learn in due course," Azarius replies.

"And you threw yourself into my path solely for these mortals?" Eiden asks, scanning our battered line. "People you barely know?"

"We share certain... common interests," Azarius says. "I look forward to traveling with them to Galamir."

"Common interests," Eiden repeats, his jaw tight. "I assume further inquiries are futile."

"Indeed."

A heavy silence hangs between the two legends. Eiden weighs weeks of irreplaceable scrying infrastructure against the satisfaction of a massacre.

"You leave me no alternative," Eiden says, his aura drawing back into his frame until the air thins to normal pressure. "I grant you your lives. For now. But know that I shall relish the hour I cut you down, Sage."

"Understood," Azarius dips his head, unfazed.

"AHAHAHAHA!" Arraka's laughter explodes from the amulet. "Serves you right, you pompous brat!"

Eiden ignores her. He turns to where Daren and I stand among the scorched stones, his closed eyes resting on us for a long, quiet moment. The breeze stirs the cooling ash between our boots.

"Well, then," Eiden says softly, the words hanging in the stillness of the road. "I suppose you two might still get that chance to prove me wrong after all..."

The space folds around him, and he vanishes without a trace.

"Phew," Azarius exhales, shaking out his sleeves. "That was rather tight, wasn't it?"

choice("Thank Azarius for his help.", B3-Ch12b-Azarius, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Azarius, v_b3_ch12_thank = 1)
choice("Tell Azarius that I could have handled myself without his help.", B3-Ch12b-Azarius, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Azarius, v_b3_ch12_thank = 0)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Azarius
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch12_thank == 1) {
"Thank you," I tell Azarius, letting out a long, shuddering breath. "I thought we were finished."

"No gratitude needed," Azarius smiles mildly. "Always glad to assist fellow participants."

"Gratitude is owed," Daren says, pushing himself up on trembling knees as white healing light washes over the raw burns across his chest. "You had everything to lose and nothing to gain by intervening. Only a man of your stature steps into that fire."

"I am sorry for earlier," Leo says, padding out from the brush with ears lowered in shame. "It was cowardly to watch from the trees while Eiden tore through you, but I had no means to alter the outcome."

"We understand, Leo," Kate says quietly, leaning heavily on her scepter as she helps Diane to her feet. "We were contracted as your bodyguards, not the reverse. This was our burden."

"I am humbled by your grace," Leo dips his head.

"Where is your apprentice?" Daren asks, glancing down the highway. "Harry, was it? Are you still traveling together?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch12_thank == 0) {
"You know I had Eiden right where I wanted him, right?" I say, sheathing my dagger with hands that won't stop trembling.

"Naturally," Azarius chuckles softly. "I saw the steel drawn and braced for the charge, but thought my method might save a little wear on the blade."

"Barry, show some respect," Daren mutters, white healing light mending the raw burns across his chest as he rises from the ditch. "Azarius risked everything to stand between us and death. He deserves our deepest thanks."

"I am sorry for earlier," Leo says, padding out from the brush with ears lowered in shame. "It was cowardly to watch from the trees while Eiden tore through you, but I had no means to alter the outcome."

"We understand, Leo," Kate says quietly, leaning heavily on her scepter as she helps Diane to her feet. "We were contracted as your bodyguards, not the reverse. This was our burden."

"I am humbled by your grace," Leo dips his head.

"Where is your apprentice?" Daren asks, glancing down the highway. "Harry, was it? Are you still traveling together?"

}
"Henry," Azarius corrects gently. "And yes, we travel together, though I left him behind before teleporting. The stakes were too lethal for a novice. He knows the route and will join us shortly."

"Forgive the suspicion after you saved our lives," Kate says, her eyes narrowing slightly, "but your timing was remarkably exact. You appeared at the precise second Eiden moved for the execution. Were you monitoring our voices from afar?"

"I was," Azarius nods cheerfully. "Astute observation."

"Enhanced auditory scrying?" I ask.

"Precisely," Azarius says.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Ending
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch12_average,1) if (v_agility == 0 && v_ancient_languages == 0 && v_aura_hardening == 0 && v_bluff == 0 && v_combat_technique == 0 && v_hearing == 0 && v_magical_sense == 0 && v_perception == 0 && v_premonition == 0 && v_reflexes == 0 && v_strength == 0 && v_toughness == 0)
achievement("(more than) Average Joe",v_ac_b3_ch12_average)
"Why wait until the final second?" Kate asks. "There was no guarantee we would survive that long. Does it tie into those 'common interests' you mentioned?"

"Perceptive as always," Azarius says. "My intervention hinged directly on a revelation voiced in the closing moments of your clash."

A breathless gasp breaks from the amulet, followed by a sudden burst of wild, hysterical laughter.

"What is so amusing, Arraka?" Daren asks, glancing at the copper disc.

"I know—ahahah—I know why the sage stepped in!" Arraka cackles, her voice practically vibrating off the metal. "Ahahaha! If it is what I think, you are going to love this!"

"Would you enlighten us, Sage Azarius?" Kate asks, her brow furrowed.

"Certainly," Azarius says, smoothing his gray robes. "Throughout my long life, I have attained fame, power, and knowledge beyond most mortal ambitions. I achieved nearly every ambition I set in my youth. Yet one final summit eludes me—the singular reason I entered this tournament."

"I may regret asking," Kate murmurs, "but what is this final ambition?"

Azarius smiles, serene and unfazed.

"To dethrone the gods and take their place."

"Aha-Ahahaha! Ahahahahahahaha!"

The wild laughter echoes across the scorched highway, ringing off the broken pines and dying away into the vast, indifferent sky of Varathia. Daren and Kate exchange a long, stunned look, the gravity of what we just survived and what lies ahead settling heavily onto our shoulders. Hadrik slowly pushes himself upright from the dust, shaking the gravel from his black beard with a low, appreciative whistle.

Azarius adjusts the folds of his gray robes, entirely untroubled, and gestures down the open cobblestone road toward the distant summits of Galamir.

Three books of running, improvising, and bleeding for every inch of ground have brought us to this quiet, sunlit ridge: battered, alive, standing beside the strongest mortal mage in the world on a march to pull down the heavens.

-----End of book 3-----

choice("Continue", B3-Ch12b-Credits, v_current_scene = B3-Ch12b-Credits)


ID: B3-Ch12b-Credits
TEXT:

 This is the end of the third Magium book.

The rest of the books have not been written yet, but they will be released on a chapter by chapter basis. (there will likely be 5 books in the main series and 2 prequels)

My current estimate is that the first chapter of the fourth book should be released within the next few months.

If you want to replay the game to find different paths, I recommend trying to complete the achievements from the main menu, because they are designed to showcase most of the important variations in the story.

If you want to chat about the game with other players while you wait for the new chapters, you can join the discussion on the r/magium subreddit, by tapping on the button below.

If you want to be notified of the game's updates, then you can visit the Magium Games twitter page, by tapping the corresponding button below, and follow me on twitter.

Also, if you want to review the game,  please keep in mind that not all players reading the game reviews have finished the game, so avoid giving spoilers in the reviews as much as possible.

Thank you for playing!

P.S. If you enjoyed the game, don't forget to recommend it to your friends.

